<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peter II, Count of Savoy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1203–<a href="/years/1268">1268</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peter II was count of Savoy from 1263, called the 'little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'. An uncle of Queen Eleanor of Provence, he spent years at the English court, where the Savoy Palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> was named after him. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Vaud</a> and extended Savoyard power north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-geneva">Lake Geneva</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Peter was born around 1203, possibly at Susa in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, probably the seventh child of Thomas I, Count of Savoy, and Margaret of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. As a younger son he began in the church, serving from 1226 to 1233 as a canon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a>, where he was briefly acting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, before he left church life in 1234. After his father died he demanded a substantial share of the county from his eldest brother Amadeus, and at Chillon in 1234 they agreed a settlement that recognised Amadeus as head of the house and gave Peter the castles of Cornillon and Angeville. His marriage to Agnes of Faucigny in 1236 brought him territory and influence in Faucigny and the Free County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Peter's conflict with his uncle William II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> ended in 1237 with a treaty obliging Geneva to pay 20,000 marks and give up the castle of Arlod. In 1244 Rudolph III, Count of Gruyère, surrendered Gruyères Castle to him. He gained key towns and trade routes in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaud">Pays de Vaud</a>, rebuilt much of the Château de Chillon, and by 1253 was protector of Bern. One scholar suggests that his conquests partly explain why French is the language of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>.</p><p>In January 1236 his niece Eleanor of Provence married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a>, and Peter went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> with other Savoyards. Henry gave him the Honour of Richmond in 1240 and knighted him on 5 January 1241; he was popularly called Earl of Richmond but never took the title. He also held the Honour of Eu and the wardship of John de Warenne, 6th Earl of Surrey, with much land in Sussex, and was granted land on the Strand by the Thames, where he built the Savoy Palace, destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peasants-revolt">Peasants' Revolt</a> of 1381. In 1258 he joined the barons who swore to support reform, but broke with them in 1260 when Simon de Montfort had him removed from the ruling council.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When his nephew Boniface, Count of Savoy, died without heirs in 1263, Peter returned and was recognised as count over Thomas III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a>, a dispute that outlasted both men. He built rounder castles, divided the county into bailis and castellanies, set up an office of accounts at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambery">Chambéry</a> and was the first count to issue laws for the whole county. Peter died without a male heir on 15 May 1268 at Pierre-Châtel and was buried at Hautecombe Abbey. His brother Philip succeeded him, and his will left his English lands to Eleanor of Provence.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peter II of Savoy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Peter II</b>, called the 'little Charlemagne', won the Vaud in the 13th century, and Chambéry became the capital in 1295.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-savoy">County of Savoy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa-piedmont">Susa</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Pierre-Châtel, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.13,7.05&amp;year=1245" aria-label="Susa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4718 570 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Peter II, Count of Savoy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5168 840h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5168 840h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.13,7.05&amp;year=1245">What was here in 1245? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
